  },
  data: {
    LLM: '1. **Trump Calling**: Eldest hand calls trumps after seeing only their first three cards. 2. **Trick Play**: Eldest leads. Players MUST follow suit if possible. 3. **Identical Card Rule**: If two or more identical cards are played to one trick, the one played FIRST wins (if it is the highest rank). 4. **Signalling**: Partners are permitted to signal suit strength via knocks (strong) or tongue-clicks (weak). 5. **Match Resolution**: If a team wins a hand, they must win the next hand with a higher trick count to end the match. If they lose the second hand, the count resets. If they win with fewer tricks, it is a draw and they must try again.',
    Player: '1. **Trump Calling**: Eldest hand calls trumps after seeing only their first three cards. 2. **Trick Play**: Eldest leads. Players MUST follow suit if possible. 3. **Identical Card Rule**: If two or more identical cards are played to one trick, the one played FIRST wins (if it is the highest rank). 4. **Signalling**: Partners are permitted to signal suit strength via knocks (strong) or tongue-clicks (weak). 5. **Match Resolution**: If a team wins a hand, they must win the next hand with a higher trick count to end the match. If they lose the second hand, the count resets. If they win with fewer tricks, it is a draw and they must try again.',
    objective: 'Win two consecutive hands (deals). The second consecutive win must contain more tricks than the first win.',
    gameplay: '1. **Trump Calling**: Eldest hand calls trumps after seeing only their first three cards. 2. **Trick Play**: Eldest leads. Players MUST follow suit if possible. 3. **Identical Card Rule**: If two or more identical cards are played to one trick, the one played FIRST wins (if it is the highest rank). 4. **Signalling**: Partners are permitted to signal suit strength via knocks (strong) or tongue-clicks (weak). 5. **Match Resolution**: If a team wins a hand, they must win the next hand with a higher trick count to end the match. If they lose the second hand, the count resets. If they win with fewer tricks, it is a draw and they must try again.',
    keyRules: [
      'Large Hands: Managing 36 cards requires physical organization and stamina.',
      'Early Trump: Calling trumps on just 3 cards introduces a massive element of luck and risk-taking.',
      'First-Card Advantage: Since multiple identical cards exist, the lead has a significant advantage in tie-breaking.',
    ],
